---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "How to Read Your Paystub: Gross Pay, Deductions, and Net Pay",
  "description": "A paystub lists gross pay, each deduction, and net pay for the period, plus year-to-date totals. Learn what each section means and which three numbers to check every payday.",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-03",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-03",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax",
    "logo": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://payrentax.me/logo.svg"
    }
  },
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "keywords": "how to read a paystub, paystub explained, paystub deductions, gross pay net pay paystub"
};
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  title="How to Read Your Paystub: Gross Pay, Deductions, and Net Pay | PayRenTax"
  description="A paystub lists gross pay, each deduction, and net pay for the period, plus year-to-date totals. Learn what each section means and which numbers to check every payday."
  canonical="/en-us/blog/how-to-read-your-paystub-2026/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <Breadcrumbs
    locale={locale}
    trail={[
      { label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') },
      { label: 'How to Read Your Paystub: Gross Pay, Deductions, and Net Pay', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/how-to-read-your-paystub-2026') }
    ]}
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900">How to Read Your Paystub: Gross Pay, Deductions, and Net Pay</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Most people glance at one number on their paystub — the amount that arrived — and
      ignore the rest. But the rest of the page is where overpayments, wrong deductions,
      and budgeting mistakes hide. It takes two minutes to read properly once you know
      what each section means.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">The header: who, when, and how often</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      The top of the stub shows your name, your employer, the pay period the stub covers,
      and the payday. Check the pay period first: if it does not match the days you
      actually worked, every number below it is suspect. The header also usually shows
      your pay rate and hours for the period, which is the starting point for everything
      else.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Gross pay: what you earned</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Gross pay is your earnings before anything is taken out: regular hours at your rate,
      plus overtime, bonuses, or commissions if they apply to this period. If your hours
      or rate changed recently, this is the first place the change should show up. A
      gross figure that does not match your hours times your rate is worth querying
      before payday confusion becomes a budgeting problem.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Deductions: where the gap goes</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      The deductions section lists everything taken out of gross pay. The usual entries
      on a US stub are federal income tax, state income tax where it applies, Social
      Security, and Medicare, followed by voluntary deductions such as health insurance
      premiums and retirement contributions. Each line should show the amount for this
      period and, separately, the year-to-date total. A deduction you do not recognise,
      or one that suddenly changed size, is the most common paystub error — flag it with
      payroll rather than assuming it will correct itself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Net pay and year-to-date totals</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Net pay, or take-home pay, is gross pay minus all deductions — the amount that
      actually reaches your account. Below it, the year-to-date columns add up every
      stub so far this year: year-to-date gross, year-to-date deductions, and
      year-to-date net. Those totals are the numbers that matter at tax time and the
      quickest way to spot a problem early, because a mistake repeated across stubs
      shows up as a year-to-date figure that does not match your own records.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">The three numbers to check every payday</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      First, gross pay against your hours and rate. Second, each deduction line against
      what you expect — especially any voluntary benefit you changed recently. Third,
      net pay against the deposit that actually arrived. If all three line up, file the
      stub and move on. If any one of them does not, you have caught the problem in the
      period it happened, when it is easiest to fix.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6">
      <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Paycheck calculator</a>
      <span class="text-slate-400"> · </span>
      <a href="/en-us/salary/paystub-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Paystub calculator</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
